It's worth adding a bit of history at this point, I think.

Many people have said Nethack likes to kill players, and that the only
way to learn what not to do is to get stung by each cause of death
once.  But in fact, the current versions of Nethack are at the end of
a long process whereby this tendency of the game has been lessened,
not increased.

Nethack's ultimate ancestor, to a degree far and above that of any
other recent roguelike, is Rogue.  This makes since, since Nethack was
derived from a game that was originally intended to be a Rogue clone.
Many things that are in the game have their origins from Rogue: much
of Rogue's bestiary remains in Nethack, nearly all of its items and
all of their special uses, the name of its major dungeon, the Big
Room, and so forth.

Rogue is a simpler game than Nethack, but it is not an easier game.
Some versions are much harder.  There are also sneaky ways to avoid
death in Rogue, but there are many fewer than Nethack.

If you take Nethack to be SuperUltraHyperRogue, then with zero
knowledge of the game world it's about as hard.  Rogue -liked- being
that hard, and many people liked Rogue.  Learning all the things that
are in Nethack make the game easier, some would say possible, but
while it might beargued that some of the additions to the game make it
too easy, most of them have been in the spirit of the clever tactics
that date back to Rogue.

And Rogue gave out no hints.  Everything in the game had to be
discovered by player experimentation, or as you would put it, learning
by dying.  A player with maximum Rogue experience is a bit better off,
but will die a lot.  A player with maximum Nethack experience, as
marvin has so memorably shown us, will win almost every time.

I do think Nethack could be a little less inscrutable about the odd
thing.  But overall, I think it needs to have its balance tightened,
making it MORE like Rogue, not less.

